{:edb/db-before-t 2791 :edb/db-after-t 2792 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536104 50 #inst "2026-10-16T02:10:07.768Z" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186059831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566213 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186059831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566262 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186059832 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566255 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186059833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566210 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186059869 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566251 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186059870 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566200 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186059870 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566231 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186068718 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566222 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186068718 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566252 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186068719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566272 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186068764 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566246 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186102776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186102777 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186102776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186102778 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186102776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186102779 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186102776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186102780 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186102776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565763 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186102776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565917 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186102776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565992 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186102776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566060 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186102776 1078 #edb/ref 17592186565992 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186105747 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566211 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186105747 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566265 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186105748 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566236 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186105809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566241 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186106150 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186106150 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n$M = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186106150 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186106150 1074 "Notice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form. The system corresponding to $M$ is \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+0y-z=1 \\\\ 0x+y+3z=2 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-z=1 \\\\ y+3z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ To do this, we apply the back substitution. \n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+3z=2\\,⇒\\,y=2-3z\n$$\n\nand from the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx-z=1\\,⇒\\,x=1+z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1+z \\\\ y=2-3z \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+z \\\\ 2-3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186107082 1064 "Let's consider a system of linear equations where the corresponding coefficient matrix is singular. For example, consider the following system and its associated augmented matrix:\n![](images/1a/1ac826685473beeb7a7c0ee90465db019d0d5b70b1af562aa3469ae4f0a54007.svg)\n\nTo solve the system, we can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as usual. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/17/176e4c49ef19bb92d63190dc6674b66ee55b2fdb306293bae65b7f2b2935701d.svg)\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n![](images/a9/a9dd77f0f2ac0f7bd04b5e70fdca3b126d4eb34b0c929caca48c60f138bc644d.svg)\n\nThe **basic variables** are the variables of our system whose coefficients are the pivots of the row echelon form matrix. Let's illustrate the pivots of $M\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/1b/1b776faa6c69553ce04fbddc4e1aa45e18d7390a32ce34d9e0317b2fa83b8752.svg)\n\nThe pivots are\n\n- $m_{11}=1,$ which is the coefficient of $x,$ and\n\n- $m_{22}=1,$ which is the coefficient of $y.$\n\nTherefore, the basic variables are $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe remaining variables are called the **free variables**. We have only one free variable in our system, which is $z$.\n\nFinally, we can describe the **general solution** by finding equations for the basic variables in terms of the free variables. In our case, this means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z$.\n\nTo do this, we apply the back substitution. From the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\ny - 3z = -5\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\ty = -5+3z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(-5+3z)+z=2\\,⟹\\, & x=-3+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n![](images/e2/e21bb2b83534152d6bf3d1c43efbd301b42717b975f1f9ebaa28ee4bc3ef31bd.svg)\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n![](images/de/de7a613bbbde25e555608ebc419106701d47875e238832eab7533bf87b0f1ce8.svg)\n\nGeometrically, this is the vector equation of a line, and any point on the line is a solution to our original system.\n\nIf we want to find some **particular solution** of the system, we simply choose some particular value of $z$ and find the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the line. For example, if we choose $z=0,$ then the corresponding values are $x=-3$ and $y=-5$ and the particular solution is $x=-3,$ $y=-5,$ $z=0.$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186107082 1064 "Let's consider a system of linear equations where the corresponding coefficient matrix is singular. For example, consider the following system and its associated augmented matrix:\n![](images/1a/1ac826685473beeb7a7c0ee90465db019d0d5b70b1af562aa3469ae4f0a54007.svg)\n\nTo solve the system, we can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as usual. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/17/176e4c49ef19bb92d63190dc6674b66ee55b2fdb306293bae65b7f2b2935701d.svg)\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n![](images/a9/a9dd77f0f2ac0f7bd04b5e70fdca3b126d4eb34b0c929caca48c60f138bc644d.svg)\n\nThe **basic variables** are the variables of our system whose coefficients are the pivots of the row echelon form matrix. Let's illustrate the pivots of $M\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/1b/1b776faa6c69553ce04fbddc4e1aa45e18d7390a32ce34d9e0317b2fa83b8752.svg)\n\nThe pivots are\n\n- $m_{11}=1,$ which is the coefficient of $x,$ and\n\n- $m_{22}=1,$ which is the coefficient of $y.$\n\nTherefore, the basic variables are $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe remaining variables are called the **free variables**. We have only one free variable in our system, which is $z$.\n\nFinally, we can describe the **general solution** by finding equations for the basic variables in terms of the free variables. In our case, this means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z$.\n\nTo do this, we apply the back substitution. From the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\ny - 3z = -5\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\ty = -5+3z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(-5+3z)+z=2\\,⟹\\, & x=-3+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n![](images/e2/e21bb2b83534152d6bf3d1c43efbd301b42717b975f1f9ebaa28ee4bc3ef31bd.svg)\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n![](images/de/de7a613bbbde25e555608ebc419106701d47875e238832eab7533bf87b0f1ce8.svg)\n\nGeometrically, this is the vector equation of a line, and any point on the line is a solution to our original system.\n\nIf we want to find some **particular solution** of the system, we simply choose some particular value of $z$ and find the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the line. For example, if we choose $z=0,$ then the corresponding values are $x=-3$ and $y=-5$ and the particular solution is $x=-3,$ $y=-5,$ $z=0.$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186110727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566212 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186110727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566249 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186121559 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121560 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186121559 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121561 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186121559 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121562 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186121559 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121563 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186121559 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565918 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186121559 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565929 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186121559 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565968 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186121559 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566024 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186121559 1078 #edb/ref 17592186121564 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186121910 1078 #edb/ref 17592186121911 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186121988 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121989 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186121988 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121990 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186121988 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121991 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186121988 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121992 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186121988 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121993 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186121988 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565711 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186121988 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565735 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186121988 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565867 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186121988 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565972 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186121988 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566017 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186121988 1078 #edb/ref 17592186565735 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124440 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566270 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124477 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566216 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124477 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566219 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124478 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566245 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124504 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566234 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124555 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566207 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124556 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566203 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124647 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566206 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124650 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566269 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566217 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124654 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566244 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124827 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124827 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $x^{2} + y^{2} - z^{2} =-1$, find its $x$-intercepts." 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186124827 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=-1$, find its $x$-intercepts." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124827 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-{0}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has no (real) solutions. \n\nTherefore, the hyperboloid does not intersect the $x$-axis." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121898 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186124885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124885 1071 "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $D$, given by\n$D = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186124885 1071 "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $D,$ given by\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124885 1074 "First, we need to convert $D$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(\\frac{2}{7}){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $D$ has $5$ pivots, and therefore the number of pivot columns is $5.$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124898 1071 "Given the hyperbolic paraboloid $(x - 5)^{2} - 8(y + 2)^{2} = z + 4$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186124898 1071 "Given the hyperbolic paraboloid $(x-5)^{2}-8(y+2)^{2}=z+4$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124898 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $y=0$ and $z=0$ in the equation of the paraboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2}-8(0+2)^{2} & =0+4 \\\\ (x-5)^{2}-32 & =4 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =36 \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm 6 \\\\ x & =5\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $x=-1$ and $x=11.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(-1,0,0)$ and $(11,0,0).$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186124923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122138 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186128960 1071 "![](images/59/5970275bd70bcfd944c03abc8f60c2b15