 we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $6$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+6\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+6\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}-5y+6) & =-({(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186184485 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186184485 1071 "Rewrite $-x^{2} - x - 3$ by completing the square." 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186184485 1071 "Rewrite $-{x}^{2}-x-3$ by completing the square." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186184485 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({x}^{2}+x+3)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x+3\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+3\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $3$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-x-3 & =-({(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186192746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566215 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186192746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566223 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186192746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566257 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186192790 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566225 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186192843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566238 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186192843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566259 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186192844 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566239 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186198411 1038 #edb/ref 17592186566263 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186233757 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566112 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186268978 1071 "Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{-4,\\,2,\\,6,\\,8\\},\\,y=\\{2,\\,-9,\\,3,\\,12\\}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186268978 1071 "Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx={-4,\\,2,\\,6,\\,8},\\,y={2,\\,-9,\\,3,\\,12}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 false] [17592186268978 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ((-4)+2+6+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (2+(-9)+3+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, th