084 #edb/ref 17592186566281 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566196 1084 #edb/ref 17592186566288 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566196 1084 #edb/ref 17592186566293 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566196 1084 #edb/ref 17592186566298 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566196 1084 #edb/ref 17592186566303 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566196 1084 #edb/ref 17592186566311 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566196 1085 "A company produces two types of products, type A and type B. The revenue function of the company, in thousands of dollars, is given by $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nwhere $x={[\\begin{aligned}\\,x & y\\,\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566196 1086 #uuid "d9a60175-ea7e-5ff5-a696-1e759f0e8e78" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566196 1087 #edb/ref 17592186566278 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566116 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566197 1069 "q-75234" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566197 1071 "Let $R=[-3,-1]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=10-4x-2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{-3,-2,-1\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566197 1072 #uuid "003282d8-d3cb-54da-a9af-399e3a75da3b" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566197 1074 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/10/10eca85fc2ab60ea0b1ee229b0a0e20110f88c4724088c0f0630d22150a8a08d.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}+{M}_{41}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=10-4x-2y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=10-4x-2y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/ea/eaa3ed265eac5a53eedc07f07e2ff386505a6ea5cc4934653d587e84238b540d.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/c3/c332690fba1843ee09bd1466f1c978c41aff8438bf9b2a23a847ca58e2f9c228.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(-3,1)=20 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(-2,1)=16 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(-3,2)=18 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(-2,2)=14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =20+16+18+14 \\\\  & =68.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566157 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566198 1069 "q-123512" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566198 1071 "Let $R=[4,5]\\times [2,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=8x-2y-1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566198 1072 #uuid "00814162-4a3c-58fe-a969-f047df1ff44b" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566198 1074 "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=8x-2y-1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=8x-2y-1$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/01/01b291ca7cd81b88cf6ed06fdcdb2ffcbb1f78255d0f57d496823d347c2fb0b3.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/a4/a44c2586978022663635d1f35399166c1ae32963fe0f4bffebd21966ad40bac5.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j})=8{x}_{i-1}-2{y}_{j}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}_{(}f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(8{x}_{i-1}-2{y}_{j}-1)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $8{x}_{i-1}-2{y}_{j}-1.$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566156 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566199 1069 "q-141139" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566199 1071 "![](images/ce/cec773f27259f88a93d2eb396319fc148a68f36311d611193b911cb2ef74162e.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is given above. Which of the following statements is true?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566199 1072 #uuid "07118ae1-aa98-5cd9-a3cf-0fa1f32e3170" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566199 1074 "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-7.$ Therefore, $x=-7$ is a *simple* root." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566129 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566200 1069 "q-61687" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566200 1071 "A radioactive substance decays exponentially such that its mass reduces by $\\frac{1}{4}$ every hour. If the mass of the substance was initially $2\\,\\text{kg},$ what is the mass of the substance after $9$ hours?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566200 1072 #uuid "09a2f783-f81f-5ba2-8e8b-78ad6330c2dd" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566200 1074 "If the mass reduces by $\\frac{1}{4},$ then $\\frac{3}{4}$ of its mass remains every hour.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ hour, the mass is $2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}).$\n\n- After $2$ hours, the mass is $2\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}.$\n\n- After $3$ hours, the mass is $2\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{3}.$\n\n- $…$\n\nRepeating this pattern, we see that the mass after $9$ hours is $2\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{9}\\,\\text{kg}.$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566165 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566201 1069 "q-38674" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566201 1071 "![](images/3b/3b22cee7a07af8745945c07bebf63fac213e26dd210b2e41762784fed64a1532.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566201 1072 #uuid "0e919c69-a07f-59d0-9a4f-80f07bde8e75" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566201 1074 "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and $x=2.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=0,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=1,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y=-5{x}^{2}(x-1)^{2}(x-2).$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566140 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566202 1069 "q-105795" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566202 1071 "Calculate $∇\\times (F-3G)$ at the point $(1,0,9)$ for the vector fields $F$ and $G,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=(2\\sqrt{z}+x)\\,i+xy\\,j+{y}^{2}\\,k,\\,(∇\\times G)(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}y\\,k.\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566202 1072 #uuid "0ea54cb1-a0d4-5539-a164-15702dc537d3" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566202 1074 "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $∇\\times (kF)=k∇\\times F$\n\n- $∇\\times (F+G)=∇\\times F+∇\\times G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (F-3G) & =∇\\times F+∇\\times (-3G) \\\\  & =∇\\times F-3∇\\times G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\times F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{z}+x & xy & {y}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2\\sqrt{z}+x)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{z}+x)]k \\\\  & =(2y-0)i-(0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}})j+(y-0)k \\\\  & =2y\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,j+y\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (F-3G) & =∇\\times F-3∇\\times G \\\\  & =2y\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,j+y\\,k-3[3{x}^{2}y\\,k] \\\\  & =2y\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,j+y\\,k-9{x}^{2}y\\,k \\\\  & =2y\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,j+y(1-9{x}^{2})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(1,0,9),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\times (F-3G))(1,0,9) & =2(0)\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}}\\,j+0(1-9(1)^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566147 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566203 1069 "q-35517" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566203 1071 "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the elliptic paraboloid $z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{8}?$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566203 1072 #uuid "0fd51843-9d70-54e0-8843-df58c7a06dd7" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566203 1074 "To find the trace in the $xz$-plane, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{8} \\\\ z & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{8} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4z-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us the equation of the parabola in $xz$-plane." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566100 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566102 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566204 1069 "q-168891" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566204 1071 "Evaluate the Hessian determinant of $f$ at $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(0,0).$\n\n$H(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)\\,=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,H(0,0)\\,=$ \n{{field-2}}" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566204 1072 #uuid "12e17457-b92e-5bbd-b375-4d92edbd2785" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566204 1074 "We computed the second partial derivatives in part 5. So, the second derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}24{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}-16 & 32xy \\\\ 32xy & 16{x}^{2}+12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n* Evaluating the second derivative at $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)=[\\begin{aligned}32 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 44\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nH(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)=32\\cdot 44-0\\cdot 0=1\\,408.\n$$\n\n* Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=-16\\cdot 12-0\\cdot 0=-192.\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566145 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566205 1069 "q-141143" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566205 1071 "![](images/67/67c87009cbafcf40172b4de6cae4cb57e37660dd15235f67500c2a0532d42dee.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements is true?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566205 1072 #uuid "13c802b0-f24f-5ef6-be2f-44ec40da3fdf" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566205 1074 "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=2.$ So, $x=2$ is a *multiple* root. \n\nFurthermore, the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=2.$ So, $x=2$ has *odd* multiplicity.\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566130 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566206 1069 "q-168888" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566206 1071 "![](images/1d/1d2132be4de3f505e5052ae3083b75ac8aceceed9f64bf62d128746a37a59a23.png)\n\nGiven a sketch of the graph of $z=f(x)$ above, which of the points ($A,$ $B,$ or $C$) is a local minimum of the function?\n\nA local minimum is the point \n{{field-1}}." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566206 1072 #uuid "187224fe-1282-5974-b5d9-f692bdbb4202" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566206 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nAmong the given options, such neighborhoods exist for the point $C.$ Hence, $C$ is a local minimum.\n\n![](images/a3/a389560d6217e86b82384443c5730168483a75a1759788ddde31ef64ad368adc.png)" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566106 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566207 1069 "q-36323" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566207 1071 "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the hyperboloid of one sheet $9{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}=1?$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566207 1072 #uuid "1a64d7fd-d80f-53bd-910a-60419647eb47" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566207 1074 "To find the trace in the $xz$-plane, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the hyperboloid. This gives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+8\\cdot {0}^{2}-4{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-4{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a hyperbola in $xz$-plane." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566148 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566208 1069 "q-123125" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566208 1071 "![](images/a4/a4ab905de5bed2182757fa11a1945bbffec175b314918b3581d2ae062d64cdcc.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566208 1072 #uuid "1acef96d-8256-5e1a-85ce-bb35520d6331" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566208 1074 "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $3$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31}).\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=\\text{Area}({R}_{31})=15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =15\\cdot {M}_{11}+15\\cdot {M}_{21}+15\\cdot {M}_{31} \\\\  & =15({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566128 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566209 1069 "q-105964" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566209 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566209 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨3z,2x,0⟩$ and the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=xz+y.$ Given that\n\n$$\n∇\\times F=⟨0,3,2⟩,\\,∇f=⟨z,1,x⟩,\n$$\n\ncalculate $∇\\times (f\\,F)$ at the point $(1,0,0).$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566209 1072 #uuid "1adbaf5f-6695-5acc-8c83-a7d990c62422" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566209 1074 "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)=f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇f\\times F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f\\times F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ z & 1 & x \\\\ 3z & 2x & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[1(0)-x(2x)]i-[z(0)-x(3z)]j+[z(2x)-1(3z)]k \\\\  & =-2{x}^{2}\\,i+3xz\\,j+(2xz-3z)\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-2{x}^{2},3xz,2xz-3z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F) & =f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F \\\\  & =(xz+y)⟨0,3,2⟩+⟨-2{x}^{2},3xz,2xz-3z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,3(xz+y),2(xz+y)⟩+⟨-2{x}^{2},3xz,2xz-3z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{x}^{2},3(xz+y)+3xz,2(xz+y)+2xz-3z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{x}^{2},6xz+3y,4xz+2y-3z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F)(1,0,0) & =⟨-2(1)^{2},6(1)(0)+3(0),4(1)(0)+2(0)-3(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566210 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566134 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566210 1069 "q-156605" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566210 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566210 1071 "What is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{310}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566210 1072 #uuid "1bb75070-fc2e-5a03-b839-07558cdd2a36" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566210 1074 "To express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{310}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${310}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/70/70325d2a1d2d252e20eea4f4940b8dc03b21c737709ffe65d95f36b51bb63fcf.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={310}^{∘}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={360}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{310}^{∘} \\\\  & ={50}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{310}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{50}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{310}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the tangent ratio is *always* negative in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{310}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{50}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566115 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566211 1069 "q-37776" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566211 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566211 1072 #uuid "209031ad-2174-5217-ad4d-29ab0216591c" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566211 1074 "Notice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form. The system corresponding to $M$ is \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y+3z=-2 \\\\ 0x+y-6z=2 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y+3z=-2 \\\\ y-6z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ To do this, we apply the back substitution. \n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny-6z=2\\,⇒\\,y=6z+2\n$$\n\nand substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx-2(6z+2)+3z=-2\\,⇒\\,x=9z+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=9z+2 \\\\ y=6z+2 \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9z+2 \\\\ 6z+2 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566123 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566212 1069 "q-223693" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566212 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=(x-5k)(x+7k)$ is $x=4.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566212 1072 #uuid "213ef30b-cee1-556f-a46a-815fdaa45856" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566212 1074 "First, we calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=4,$ we will set our result equal to $4.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=(x-5k)(x+7k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=5k$ and ${x}_{2}=-7k.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5k+(-7k)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2k}{2} \\\\  & =-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-k & =4 \\\\ k & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566149 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566213 1069 "q-156075" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566213 1071 "What is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{197}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566213 1072 #uuid "27716467-fe24-5b2e-a5ee-4be993036021" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566213 1074 "To express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{197}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${197}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/d4/d483d80833650a101f993d9f66ffb9c786681240a4bfa4e255fb116cc7ad5fd2.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={197}^{∘}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\theta -{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={197}^{∘}-{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={17}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{197}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{17}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{197}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{197}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{17}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566183 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566214 1069 "q-40012" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566214 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{3}{4x-2}?$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566214 1072 #uuid "29510dc5-be54-5010-a9b8-7e994b64e473" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566214 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566127 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566215 1069 "q-155564" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566215 1071 "Represent the solution of the inequality $2>3x+5$ on a number line." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566215 1072 #uuid "29a403df-d998-5649-bdb5-51e39508e73b" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566215 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $5$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & >3x+5 \\\\ 2-5 & >3x+5-5 \\\\ -3 & >3x+0 \\\\ -3 & >3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & >3x \\\\ \\frac{-3}{3} & >\\frac{3x}{3} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{3} & >\\frac{\\cancel{3}x}{\\cancel{3}} \\\\ -1 & >x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember, we also need to flip the inequality.\n\n$$\nx<-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x<-1.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](images/09/093f2e5ade9371ae84fa09ab6bee24b18930a781aac7a5fd16c3dd35fecae5ab.png)" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566105 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566216 1069 "q-73605" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566216 1071 "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $B?$\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566216 1072 #uuid "29b4d870-1dc8-5b15-b7e0-9a157e214c86" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566216 1074 "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566152 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566159 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566172 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566217 1069 "q-168889" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566217 1071 "The critical points of $f$ are$\\,$$(\\pm $ \n{{field-1}}\n$,$ \n{{field-2}}\n$)$ and $(0,$ \n{{field-3}}\n$).$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566217 1072 #uuid "2ed0a30c-70d1-57fa-8ba9-ce180a14b5a1" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566217 1074 "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x & \\,16{x}^{2}y+12y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x=0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}y+12y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}8x({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2)=0 \\\\ 4y(4{x}^{2}+3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $4{x}^{2}+3\\ne 0,$ from the second equation of the system, we obtain that\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(0,0).$\n\nNote that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these points, they are also stationary points." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566138 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566218 1069 "q-164124" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566218 1071 "Let $R=[0,1]\\times [1,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x.$ \nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566218 1072 #uuid "2f8d79c3-b613-54e6-b2e9-de270331920a" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566218 1074 "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x$ increases as $x$ increases on $[0,1]$, and\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x$ increases as $y$ increases on $[1,2].$\n\n![](images/67/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/51/51808df96cf7dd295bfa24aa3641a601c821116e1a0014898ffe705036704a53.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j-1})={x}_{i-1}^{2}+{y}_{j-1}^{2}+2{x}_{i-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}^{2}+{y}_{j-1}^{2}+2{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is ${x}_{i-1}^{2}+{y}_{j-1}^{2}+2{x}_{i-1}.$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566163 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566219 1069 "q-130933" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566219 1071 "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $B?$\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -7 & 4 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566219 1072 #uuid "389b06ef-bd0c-5b5c-b775-c400f3aed0c2" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566219 1074 "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $5$th columns:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -7 & 4 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566181 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566220 1069 "q-141135" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566220 1071 "![](images/d3/d32c9f722bee1a6391c943335c0024be2237aa1779760013fa368508bc8473b6.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is given above. Which of the following statements is true?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566220 1072 #uuid "38e88fc8-0fa8-5760-aae8-838a940ddec3" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566220 1074 "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=4.$ Therefore, $x=4$ is a *simple* root." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566150 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566221 1069 "q-131475" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566221 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x{e}^{y},yz,0⟩$ and the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{-y}.$ Given that\n\n$$\n∇\\times F=⟨-y,0,-x{e}^{y}⟩,\\,∇f=⟨{e}^{-y},-x{e}^{-y},0⟩,\n$$\n\ncalculate $∇\\times (f\\,F)$ at the point $(0,1,2).$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566221 1072 #uuid "38f95650-79fd-557c-9c72-45e47bd9ea35" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566221 1074 "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)=f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇f\\times F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f\\times F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {e}^{-y} & -x{e}^{-y} & 0 \\\\ x{e}^{y} & yz & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[(-x{e}^{-y})(0)-(yz)(0)]i-[({e}^{-y})(0)-(x{e}^{y})(0)]j+[({e}^{-y})(yz)-(x{e}^{y})(-x{e}^{-y})]k \\\\  & =0\\,i+0j+(yz{e}^{-y}+{x}^{2})k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,yz{e}^{-y}+{x}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F) & =f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F \\\\  & =(x{e}^{-y})⟨-y,0,-x{e}^{y}⟩+⟨0,0,yz{e}^{-y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-xy{e}^{-y},0,-{x}^{2}⟩+⟨0,0,yz{e}^{-y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-xy{e}^{-y},0,yz{e}^{-y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)(0,1,2)=⟨0,0,\\frac{2}{e}⟩.\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566111 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566117 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566160 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566222 1069 "q-215477" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566222 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $|\\frac{1}{4}-x|<\\frac{7}{4}.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x$\n{{field-3}}\n$2$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566222 1072 #uuid "3b6c8eac-bc2e-56d5-9974-3113a1a1870d" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566222 1074 "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $\\frac{1}{4}-x$ whose distance from zero is less than $\\frac{7}{4}.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{7}{4}<\\frac{1}{4}-x<\\frac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{4}<\\frac{1}{4}-x & <\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ -2<-x & <\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2>x & >-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the final result easier to read, we will rewrite it so that the smaller number is on the left:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3}{2}<x<2\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566168 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566223 1069 "q-101945" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566223 1071 "Represent the solution of the inequality $2x+9>1$ on a number line." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566223 1072 #uuid "3b73619e-ed22-51ef-83f4-ae6eed9f4cb4" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566223 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $9$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+9 & >1 \\\\ 2x+9-9 & >1-9 \\\\ 2x+0 & >-8 \\\\ 2x & >-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & >-8 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & >\\frac{-8}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & >\\frac{-8}{2} \\\\ x & >-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x>-4.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c135701f345826b8ae0ba0cc8dab059834a7e7b968bc87067af45a2622d0514c.png)" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566151 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566224 1069 "q-105796" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566224 1071 "Calculate $∇\\times (F+G)$ at the point $(0,-1,1)$ for the vector fields $F$ and $G,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i+3z\\,j-2y\\,\\text{k},\\,(∇\\times G)(x,y,z)=(6z-1)\\,j.\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566224 1072 #uuid "3c0d5e56-2169-54d3-9966-1a109d2d5523" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566224 1074 "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $∇\\times (kF)=k∇\\times F$\n\n- $∇\\times (F+G)=∇\\times F+∇\\times G$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\times F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & 3z & -2y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3z)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)]k \\\\  & =(-2-3)i-(0-0)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =-5\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (F+G) & =∇\\times F+∇\\times G \\\\  & =-5\\,i+(6z-1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(0,-1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\times (F+G))(0,-1,1) & =-5\\,i+(6(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-5\\,i+5\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566141 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566225 1069 "q-168886" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566225 1071 "$\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,=${{field-1}}" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566225 1072 #uuid "4d7ebf34-a035-5c2e-ad76-841acaa3c221" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566225 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the constant multiple rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}) & =2\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4})+8{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})-8\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4{x}^{3}+8{y}^{2}\\cdot 2x-8\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566124 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566226 1069 "q-123232" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566226 1071 "Let $R=[1,5]\\times [0,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=10-x-{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,1\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566226 1072 #uuid "50cce965-116a-5091-8060-d4b135e273bd" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566226 1074 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/5b/5bd8200cb0f0aa8ca38548ae731acb54d7118e3ca735ebd5e4aebe5b6a7aa1b5.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31})+{M}_{41}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{41}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}+{M}_{41}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=10-x-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=10-x-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/cc/cc616280fc457f14441e30a9e551eacf73bd56de02e68ced200302cbb1db68ac.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/e2/e2e1ab24e5d73cfc6414d7e01cd2d2d923f0f73bca137e65c9968b9142360389.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,0)=9 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(2,0)=8 \\\\ {M}_{31} & =f(3,0)=7 \\\\ {M}_{41} & =f(4,0)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =9+8+7+6 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566132 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566227 1069 "q-140407" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566227 1071 "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}+9y-21$ by completing the square." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566227 1072 #uuid "52455c8c-f427-542e-9cf2-e8b407fdf74f" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566227 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-9y+21)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-9y+21\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+21\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $21$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+21\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+21\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+9y-21 & =-({(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566110 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566228 1069 "q-118400" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566228 1071 "Which of the following is the net of a rectangular pyramid?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566228 1072 #uuid "533214ac-e847-5fb2-8a57-a2469ce2936c" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566228 1074 "Let's start by portraying our rectangular pyramid:\n\n![](images/8a/8a0f95e29e6cbf6afdb2cfad3d2f8fe7bfd367769b29571417e514071684055d.png)\n\n  \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nIf we were to unfold the rectangular pyramid above along its edges, we'd get the net shown below.  \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n \n\n![](images/04/0431c3f2e85a9a1547e9d528926da076da6f087214be4720448a3a4b73301c35.png)\n\n    \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/09/09d5c918414e9df385ff9db8fd21f9626ef34dabaee6e67296194b57e92984e0.png)" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566122 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566229 1069 "q-70812" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566229 1071 "![](images/75/75b9d9a0500f526670c59b4f92c28f4da8d979e39c615ae4f25ec960a80ca1e0.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $x=0$ is a root of odd multiplicity.\n\nII. $x=0$ is a simple root.\n\nIII. $p(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566229 1072 #uuid "571594cb-dae3-572c-bed1-7b71f8315ee0" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566229 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=0$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=0$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement II is false. The root $x=0$ is not a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at that point, and the curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ moves right in the positive direction towards $+\\infty ,$ the $y$ value moves up in the positive direction towards $+\\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566191 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566230 1069 "q-75327" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566230 1071 "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [3,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=140-4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566230 1072 #uuid "577873fb-8648-59ce-9de9-8ad820b016f9" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566230 1074 "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=140-4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases on $[1,3]$, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=140-4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases on $[3,4].$\n\n![](images/ce/ce0d90127e1f14654f4b32a933f1eb6e76e054c03f19ca40c3f04116aa3c2c93.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/4a/4a10c8332a5f346d393c37531c13db6aa69d4cecb241efd7c34d57f32d5a98b8.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j})=140-4{x}_{i}^{2}-6{y}_{j}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(140-4{x}_{i}^{2}-6{y}_{j}^{2})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $140-4{x}_{i}^{2}-6{y}_{j}^{2}.$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566192 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566231 1069 "q-61703" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566231 1071 "A soccer club started with $120$ members. The number of members in the club then decreased by $\\frac{1}{5}$ every year. Which expression gives approximately the number of members after $3$ years?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566231 1072 #uuid "5c6f292d-8813-58fc-b4b8-7f51b18fc102" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566231 1074 "If the number of members reduced by $\\frac{1}{5}$ every year, it means that $\\frac{4}{5}$ of members remained every year.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ year, there were $120\\cdot (\\frac{4}{5})$ members.\n\n- After $2$ years, there were approximately $120\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}$ members.\n\n- After $3$ years, there were approximately $120\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{3}$ members." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566126 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566154 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566164 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566232 1069 "q-218758" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566232 1071 "Rewrite $10{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{7}{10}$ by completing the square.\n\n$10{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{7}{10}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566232 1072 #uuid "5eff477c-d77b-5edc-9e57-fb4474642e96" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566232 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $10.$ So, we take out a factor of $10$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n10({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{7}{100}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}x+{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{50}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}) & =10({(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{50}) \\\\  & =10{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+10\\cdot \\frac{3}{50} \\\\  & =10{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566170 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566233 1069 "q-75321" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566233 1071 "Let $R=[3,7]\\times [0,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+7{y}^{2}-10.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566233 1072 #uuid "5f9a41d3-0af2-58dd-af4a-729df4a947b7" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566233 1074 "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+7{y}^{2}-10$ increases as $x$ increases on $[3,7],$ and\n\n- $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+7{y}^{2}-10$ increases as $y$ increases on $[0,4].$\n\n![](images/67/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/51/51808df96cf7dd295bfa24aa3641a601c821116e1a0014898ffe705036704a53.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j-1})=2{x}_{i-1}^{2}+7{y}_{j-1}^{2}-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(2{x}_{i-1}^{2}+7{y}_{j-1}^{2}-10)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $2{x}_{i-1}^{2}+7{y}_{j-1}^{2}-10.$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566136 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566234 1069 "q-164268" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566234 1071 "Given the elliptic paraboloid $z+8=\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6}$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566234 1072 #uuid "606d371f-fe47-59ac-b0e1-18679ee51770" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566234 1074 "To find the $y$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+8 & =\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{4}{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6} \\\\ 8 & =2+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6} & =6 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =36 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $y=-7,5.$ \n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-7,0)$ and $(0,5,0).$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566175 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566235 1069 "q-134161" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566235 1071 "Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{4,\\,4,\\,-4,\\,4\\},\\,y=\\{-1,\\,-2,\\,7,\\,4\\}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566235 1072 #uuid "64eb2650-1408-5539-acdf-62ad07765696" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566235 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (4+4+(-4)+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ((-1)+(-2)+7+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-2)(-1-2)+(4-2)(-2-2)+(-4-2)(7-2)+(4-2)(4-2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2)(-3)+(2)(-4)+(-6)(5)+(2)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-6-8-30+4] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-40) \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566133 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566236 1069 "q-73916" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566236 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -5 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -12 & -20 & -24 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{5}{2} & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566236 1072 #uuid "6a78aa41-fab4-5422-8e61-82dda2111dae" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566236 1074 "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -5 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -12 & -20 & -24 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{5}{2} & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -5 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{5}{2} & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -5 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y-5z=-6 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove these equations from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\nx-3y-5z=-6\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables $y$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y,z.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\nx=-6+3y+5z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-6+3y+5z \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y,z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6+3y+5z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566143 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566237 1069 "q-123056" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566237 1071 "![](images/6d/6dcc08ca41977f189d4e873be6c060b3d8f446fb3ba2e4b0ee2667403ebb07fc.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566237 1072 #uuid "6b6196d0-946b-5675-b1cf-30fba169b2db" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566237 1074 "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31})+{M}_{41}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{41}).\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=\\text{Area}({R}_{31})=\\text{Area}({R}_{41})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =2\\cdot {M}_{11}+2\\cdot {M}_{21}+2\\cdot {M}_{31}+2\\cdot {M}_{41} \\\\  & =2({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}+{M}_{41}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566120 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566146 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566238 1069 "q-176017" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566238 1071 "The solution to the inequality $-4x+5<-7$ is $x${{field-1}}{{field-2}}." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566238 1072 #uuid "6bc91ea0-3fcd-5fed-9f63-f013dbd34da2" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566238 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $5$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+5 & <-7 \\\\ -4x+5-5 & <-7-5 \\\\ -4x+0 & <-12 \\\\ -4x & <-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-4.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x\\,\\, & <\\,\\,-12 \\\\ \\frac{-4x}{-4}\\, & >\\,\\,\\frac{-12}{-4} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-4}x}{\\cancel{-4}}\\, & >\\,\\,3 \\\\ x\\,\\, & >\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x>3.$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566125 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566239 1069 "q-154330" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566239 1071 "Solve the inequality $8>-3y-1.$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566239 1072 #uuid "6cb83115-237f-5265-8ee0-e3661c1aa677" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566239 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $1$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & >-3y-1 \\\\ 8+1 & >-3y-1+1 \\\\ 9 & >-3y+0 \\\\ 9 & >-3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-3.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-3y \\\\ \\frac{9}{-3}\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{-3y}{-3} \\\\ -3\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{\\cancel{-3}y}{\\cancel{-3}} \\\\ -3\\, & <\\,\\,y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember, we also need to flip the inequality.\n\n$$\ny>-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y>-3.$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566166 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566240 1069 "q-140530" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566240 1071 "Rewrite $4{x}^{2}+32x+40$ by completing the square." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566240 1072 #uuid "6fcb379b-2e62-54ea-ab65-5d0b75f0dbcd" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566240 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, we take out a factor of $4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n4({x}^{2}+8x+10)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+8x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-{4}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-6\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+8x+10) & =4((x+4)^{2}-6) \\\\  & =4{(x+4)}^{2}-4\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =4{(x+4)}^{2}-24\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566187 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566241 1069 "q-131507" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566241 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 8 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 9 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566241 1072 #uuid "70a3bd24-6afb-575f-802d-3c1798798cee" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566241 1074 "The system corresponding to $M$ is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}7x+8y-7z=5 \\\\ 0x-5y+9z=-8 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are no such numbers $x,y,z$ that form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=1,$ we can conclude that our system has no solution." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566108 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566242 1069 "q-140322" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566242 1071 "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-12x+35)$ by completing the square." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566242 1072 #uuid "71c17160-e343-5b78-8eee-e55ba4967a40" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566242 1074 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-12x+35\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+35\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $35$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-12x+35) & =-({(x-6)}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =-{(x-6)}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-(x-6)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566144 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566243 1069 "q-52543" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566243 1071 "Rewrite $-2{x}^{2}-6x$ by completing the square." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566243 1072 #uuid "71daf213-45ae-576f-97ae-9ffbff2f758a" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566243 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({x}^{2}+3x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}+3x) & =-2({(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566118 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566178 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566185 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566244 1069 "q-168892" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566244 1071 "Classify the critical points using the second partial derivatives test.\n\n$(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ are local minima since $\\,H(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$\n{{field-1}}\n$0\\,\\,$ and $\\,\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$\n{{field-2}}\n$0$\n\n$(0,0)$ is a saddle point since $\\,\\,H(0,0)$\n{{field-3}}\n$0$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566244 1072 #uuid "75a986c0-75fe-5567-a170-fed953f3d35d" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566244 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n* If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n  * if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$ \n\n  * if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n* If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n* If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nIn part 4, we found the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(0,0).$ Their corresponding Hessian determinants and the second partial derivatives at these points were computed in part 6.\n\nWith that in mind, let's classify our critical points:\n\n* $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ are local minima of $f$ since $H(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)=1\\,408>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)=32>0.$\n\n* $(0,0)$ is a saddle point since $H(0,0)=-192<0.$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566167 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566245 1069 "q-73615" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566245 1071 "The row echelon form matrix $A$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566245 1072 #uuid "75f69126-c110-5545-9efd-4ceeba235a0c" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566245 1074 "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $A$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $2$nd and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566162 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566246 1069 "q-14467" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566246 1071 "$\\frac{9{v}^{2}+3v}{v+9}÷(3v+1)=$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566246 1072 #uuid "7688caa9-079d-576d-afcf-e01cab97b9fe" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566246 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{v}^{2}+3v}{v+9}÷(3v+1),\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9{v}^{2}+3v}{v+9}÷(3v+1) & = \\\\ \\frac{9{v}^{2}+3v}{v+9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(3v+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3v(3v+1)}{v+9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(3v+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3v\\cancel{(3v+1)}}{(v+9)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{(3v+1)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3v}{v+9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566161 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566247 1069 "q-61779" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566247 1071 "![](images/56/5683cb4560a5715654c795d94f4123c79a758e281ef40b92cccb379482506c2c.png)\n\nWhich of the statements is true about the solids depicted above?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566247 1072 #uuid "776cd40d-1c79-55ee-9771-1ee0329ddc24" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566247 1074 "Let's sketch the nets of each solid.\n\n![](images/27/277d5aa8a8116d230bf000ef7d8e94a071acbf499a49b9d8b911a4e89cfaf18a.png)\n\n \n\nAs we can see, the correct answer is:\n\n$\\,$ *\"The net of II consists of triangles only\"*\n\nOther statements are false. \n\n- *\"The net of III consists of triangles only\"* is false since the net of III has one rectangle (the base of the solid).\n\n- *\"The net of I consists of $3$ triangles and $3$ rectangles\"* is not true because the net of I contains only $2$ triangles (the base and the top of the solid).\n\n- *\"The net of III consists of $3$ triangles and $1$ rectangle\"* is false since the net of III has $4$ triangles (the side faces).\n\n- *\"The net of II consists of $3$ triangles and $1$ rectangle\"* is not true because the net of II does not contain rectangles." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566113 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566248 1069 "q-62035" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566248 1071 "![](images/f5/f5be9bb97784c25d555d4581bc67916ab0b8e57ade33619b3df27d2eb0f59db2.png)\n\nThe net of which polyhedron is shown above?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566248 1072 #uuid "7f868203-0afb-5c74-9f79-f3b6b4ea5865" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566248 1074 "![](images/d9/d9010650127d8dcfcd387df09b9dce689ece90e3912ede882241c8b714f91d45.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nIf we were to fold the net along its edges to form a solid shape, we'd get a triangular pyramid.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/6b/6bf9ac2bd130fdad3a056d0e5680e010003366f115e3655de59bbd795d1513f5.png)" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566171 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566249 1069 "q-220358" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566249 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=(2x+k)(x-3k)$ is $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566249 1072 #uuid "83c46e33-f8bd-5862-a614-a54a16643b82" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566249 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we will set our result equal to $\\frac{1}{4}.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=(2x+k)(x-3k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{k}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=3k.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{k}{2}+3k)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{6k-k}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5k}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5k}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 5k & =1 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566142 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566250 1069 "q-164126" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566250 1071 "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=160-{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566250 1072 #uuid "8e6c279a-ebea-5cc3-9684-5647027801a8" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566250 1074 "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=160-{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases on $[0,2]$, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=160-{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases on $[1,3].$\n\n![](images/ce/ce0d90127e1f14654f4b32a933f1eb6e76e054c03f19ca40c3f04116aa3c2c93.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/4a/4a10c8332a5f346d393c37531c13db6aa69d4cecb241efd7c34d57f32d5a98b8.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j})=160-{x}_{i}^{2}-2{y}_{j}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(160-{x}_{i}^{2}-2{y}_{j}^{2})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $160-{x}_{i}^{2}-2{y}_{j}^{2}.$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566153 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566251 1069 "q-61861" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566251 1071 "Nathan invested $\\$5\\,600$ into a company which then went on to lose $7%$ of its value every year. Which expression gives the value of Nathan's investment after $3$ years?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566251 1072 #uuid "92938c74-0d42-5181-8d49-61141e646c18" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566251 1074 "The number $7%$ (expressed as a decimal) is equal to $0.07.$ Therefore, a decrease of $7%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of:\n\n$$\n1-0.07=0.93\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ year, the value of the investment was $5\\,600\\cdot (0.93)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the value of the investment was $5\\,600\\cdot (0.93)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the value of the investment was $5\\,600\\cdot (0.93)^{3}$ dollars." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566104 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566131 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566176 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566252 1069 "q-212475" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566252 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $|9-6t|\\le 15.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$t\\le $\n{{field-3}}" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566252 1072 #uuid "92ccb6ea-9886-5668-a071-d63ea68855d0" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566252 1074 "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $9-6t$ whose distance from zero is less than or equal to $15.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-15\\le 9-6t\\le 15\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15\\le 9-6t & \\le 15 \\\\ -24\\le -6t & \\le 6 \\\\ 24\\ge 6t & \\ge -6 \\\\ 4\\ge t & \\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the final result easier to read, we will rewrite it so that the smaller number is on the left:\n\n$$\n-1\\le t\\le 4\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566193 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566253 1069 "q-118420" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566253 1071 "Which of the following is **not** the net of a prism?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566253 1072 #uuid "95eb11a1-9c88-5c12-9e33-b81f6a14bd8e" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566253 1074 "![](images/e7/e7017b17e88561231e151d48fe5337bc82d164639f785eda0b0b148c9ee80b37.png)\n\nIf we were to fold the net above along its edges, then we'd get a rectangular pyramid, as shown below:\n\n![](images/5e/5e32ef37ef2dae4c714afcb744820c8fb1cbbc3bd23f7347552b3a7bc73949bc.png)\n\nSo, the net that does not correspond to a prism is:\n\n![](images/c3/c35316b772366403fca0d0ba030866a82ef0d7c411c2817aa7e1feffa894c96e.png)\n\n   \n\nThe rest of the given nets will give us a (triangular or rectangular) prism." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566184 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566254 1069 "q-75304" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566254 1071 "Let $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+4y+5.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566254 1072 #uuid "9fe67796-b4b4-5e92-895d-48a77cf8ca60" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566254 1074 "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+4y+5$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+4y+5$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/67/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/51/51808df96cf7dd295bfa24aa3641a601c821116e1a0014898ffe705036704a53.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j-1})={x}_{i-1}+4{y}_{j-1}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+4{y}_{j-1}+5)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is ${x}_{i-1}+4{y}_{j-1}+5.$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566121 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566255 1069 "q-124455" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566255 1071 "What is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566255 1072 #uuid "a1630114-3830-5e2d-b973-4d57fd87afe3" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566255 1074 "To express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${140}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/5f/5f2b641a3a765f65f1d7d11837193341edb6876dfaea45e9eeaf1d7610178435.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={140}^{∘}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={180}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{140}^{∘} \\\\  & ={40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{40}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the cosine ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{40}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566189 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566256 1069 "q-52539" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566256 1071 "Rewrite $7{x}^{2}-21x$ by completing the square." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566256 1072 #uuid "a3f44aac-b56c-541f-849d-0822403501d2" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566256 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $7.$ So, we take out a factor of $7$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n7({x}^{2}-3x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7({x}^{2}-3x) & =7({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-7\\cdot \\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{63}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566179 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566257 1069 "q-155559" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566257 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566257 1071 "Represent the solution of the inequality $5x-3<2$ on a number line." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566257 1072 #uuid "b5942d8a-a361-5a4c-829b-f23ceb66bd8b" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566257 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $3$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-3 & <2 \\\\ 5x-3+3 & <2+3 \\\\ 5x+0 & <5 \\\\ 5x & <5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & <5 \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & <\\frac{5}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}x}{\\cancel{5}} & <\\frac{5}{5} \\\\ x & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x<1.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c1f7c992d9df85434eaf96e5bd9af52f9b6228e42fede6e9d4001e6471c3c239.png)" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566194 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566258 1069 "q-123058" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566258 1071 "![](images/dd/dd73cb7785de514503cba6a04931a88084172b58826efdf22ea847f256c375fb.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566258 1072 #uuid "b7b6e192-3a06-52fc-8d5f-7a6810288b2c" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566258 1074 "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $2$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12}).\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =4\\cdot {M}_{11}+4\\cdot {M}_{12} \\\\  & =4({M}_{11}+{M}_{12}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566103 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566155 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566259 1069 "q-176019" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566259 1071 "The solution to the inequality $-2x+10\\ge 4$ is $x${{field-1}}{{field-2}}." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566259 1072 #uuid "b84140b5-0b6b-54d3-81ed-16117dd9ce00" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566259 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $10$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+10 & \\ge 4 \\\\ -2x+10-10 & \\ge 4-10 \\\\ -2x+0 & \\ge -6 \\\\ -2x & \\ge -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-2.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-6 \\\\ \\frac{-2x}{-2}\\, & \\le \\,\\,\\frac{-6}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-2}x}{\\cancel{-2}}\\, & \\le \\,\\,3 \\\\ x\\,\\, & \\le \\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\le 3.$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566186 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566260 1069 "q-140319" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566260 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566260 1071 "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}-8y+60)$ by completing the square." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566260 1072 #uuid "bb8a5938-134a-51c9-838d-3e4010a8ca50" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566260 1074 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-8y+60\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+60\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $60$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-{4}^{2}+60\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+60\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}+44.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}-8y+60) & =-({(y-4)}^{2}+44) \\\\  & =-{(y-4)}^{2}-44 \\\\  & =-44-(y-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566137 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566261 1069 "q-62017" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566261 1071 "![](images/41/416f9df81e2159b58bddde964435f97fac3e461ce984402c2ea22616bc0edd48.png)\n\nThe net of which polyhedron is shown above?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566261 1072 #uuid "bcd25659-0e9d-5735-91bb-5b7182253798" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566261 1074 "![](images/b2/b2ea30cd84f4d52f5d1e4a910817399c892c38a4b87ec7773eace67000a865fd.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nIf we were to fold the net along its edges to form a solid shape, we'd get a rectangular pyramid.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/a0/a097a94ed0fa5ed23cf4e9b2ae36319ee2a00fb276b8b0bd1fe21999c1887f0f.png)" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566188 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566262 1069 "q-124446" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566262 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566262 1071 "What is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{245}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566262 1072 #uuid "bd9a81c2-dd21-5cb3-9488-a8b270b5ad9b" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566262 1074 "To express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{245}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${245}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/31/312fc3581d9da413254a0eb4dbc64091b286675492821838ba9a65ee06f45a7c.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={245}^{∘}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\theta -{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={245}^{∘}-{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={65}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{245}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{65}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{245}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{245}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{65}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566101 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566263 1069 "q-168887" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566263 1071 "${f}^{′}(x)=$$[\\,8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x$ \n{{field-1}}\n$]$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566263 1072 #uuid "c7c3402d-fbe9-5875-8d06-b63e54fe220e" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566263 1074 "For our scalar function, we have that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={(∇f)}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEarlier, we found that \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x.\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-1)=16{x}^{2}y+12y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x\\, & \\,16{x}^{2}y+12y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566114 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566264 1069 "q-52532" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566264 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566264 1071 "Rewrite $-4{x}^{2}+8x$ by completing the square." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566264 1072 #uuid "d53c2052-a67a-5988-8f1a-4c9a720c7a75" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566264 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-4.$ So, we take out a factor of $-4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-4({x}^{2}-2x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-1)}^{2}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-1)}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4({x}^{2}-2x) & =-4({(x-1)}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =-4{(x-1)}^{2}+(-4)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-4{(x-1)}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566182 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566265 1069 "q-131487" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566265 1071 "Consider the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=-8 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566265 1072 #uuid "d941de1f-e5bc-5cb7-8728-2a74f1c72d5f" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566265 1074 "The augmented matrix for the system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 3 & -12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 3 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 3 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 3 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566107 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566266 1069 "q-123243" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566266 1071 "Let $R=[0,5]\\times [1,5]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=100-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,5\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,3,5\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566266 1072 #uuid "e0e69171-a8d5-534f-acb8-6c56c972aa07" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566266 1074 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/77/77fdf238fb2b4046da6ddc69024378a56e4de5f46de301d5fac094cbc290b23b.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=10$ for both subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =10\\cdot {M}_{11}+10\\cdot {M}_{12} \\\\  & =10({M}_{11}+{M}_{12}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=100-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=100-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/62/62871cd2fcc4993e0775fb7bdf0609d7849a1aa2ea5a314bc727967df590d6e7.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/9b/9b814374a5b132560656d6fe2dc2350cb693847733a40c0d21b9a13be30025e1.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(0,1)=97 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(0,3)=73\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =10(97+73) \\\\  & =1\\,700.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566139 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566267 1069 "q-75184" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566267 1071 "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+5.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566267 1072 #uuid "e4fe7c63-ddd1-59b8-9a3b-8f1a0e46d1f0" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566267 1074 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/6f/6fc1abc4b93b59eda024e126f8503b23f067bd9ea086a0256fcd3e67dfd7200a.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+5$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+5$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/7d/7dbec95d53ae55a09ab0a941c69f506f11be6e645a03aca3095bede1b3e966f8.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/5e/5edcabe01ac87d115a69a8b842c537187b1f1d2a003664e5fb68f7e70db3ae3d.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(2,2)=17 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(3,2)=27 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(2,3)=22 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(3,3)=32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =17+27+22+32 \\\\  & =98.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566109 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566268 1069 "q-25252" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566268 1071 "Rewrite $-{a}^{2}-10a+16$ by completing the square." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566268 1072 #uuid "e52c4055-fdc4-580f-9736-b9940edca056" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566268 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({a}^{2}+10a-16)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}+10a-16\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}+2\\cdot 5a-16\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $16$ last:\n\n$$\n{{a}^{2}+2\\cdot 5a+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-{5}^{2}-16\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(a+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}-16\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(a+5)^{2}-41\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-10a-{a}^{2} & =-((a+5)^{2}-41) \\\\  & =-(a+5)^{2}+41 \\\\  & =41-(a+5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566180 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566269 1069 "q-71858" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566269 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(z-3)^{2}}{9}=1$, find its $y$-intercepts." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566269 1072 #uuid "e6548717-af2d-5bfc-9298-8a16a46973b2" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566269 1074 "To find the $y$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 1+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}-1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ y & =2\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $y=0$ and $y=4.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,4,0).$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566169 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566173 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566174 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566270 1069 "q-168890" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566270 1071 "Find the second partial derivatives of $f.$\n\n$\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\,=$\n{{field-2}}\n\n$\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}=\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}\\,=$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566270 1072 #uuid "ed3eb4a9-71c7-55f2-a5c3-dcaf4e7245dc" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566270 1074 "Previously, we found that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=16{x}^{2}y+12y.\n$$\n\nComputing the second partial derivatives, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x)=24{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}-16 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(16{x}^{2}y+12y)=32xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(16{x}^{2}y+12y)=16{x}^{2}+12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566119 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566271 1069 "q-109851" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566271 1071 "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Null}(B)$ for every matrix $B?$\n\nI. $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(B)$\n\nIII. $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566271 1072 #uuid "f11ffdfd-619b-56d9-be79-9ad5a11a6df9" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566271 1074 "Recall that $\\text{Null}(B)$ contains all vectors that, when pre-multiplied by $B,$ result in the zero-vector $0.$ \n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by $B,$ we actually multiply each row of $B$ by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}(B)$ must be orthogonal to the row space of $B,$ which is $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $B$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18713ed3d103ca0a02d74cede1e6448012e0aa28a2df804d41cfb277589a0158.png)" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566158 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566272 1069 "q-29945" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566272 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $|3-2x|>3.$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566272 1072 #uuid "f551ab94-4892-58d5-baeb-a476fa14edd7" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566272 1074 "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $3-2x$ whose distance from zero is greater than $3.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-2x & <-3 & \\text{or} &  & 3-2x & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-2x & <-3 &  &  & 3-2x & >3 \\\\ -2x & <-6 &  &  & -2x & >0 \\\\ x & >3 &  &  & x & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <0 & \\text{or} &  & x & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566177 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566273 1069 "q-163179" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566273 1071 "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}$\nand has mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nGiven that the center of mass is at $(1,2),$ find the moment of inertia about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566273 1072 #uuid "f7c6f3ba-6533-5a1f-b464-492440ca8f6d" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566273 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =x{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass $(1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}((x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(x-1)^{2}y+{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}+4y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}-\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}+2{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{9}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}(x-1)^{3}+\\frac{9}{4}x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}-(-\\frac{3}{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566190 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566274 1069 "q-75045" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566274 1071 "Let $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y+2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{-1,0,1\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{-1,0,1\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566274 1072 #uuid "f88ad5cd-8533-5739-b1c9-9a040c2e3b2c" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566274 1074 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/0c/0c68391a7a23867f21ee851aa86d389ff4dc186c3aaedde0ccb1fe15b5b4ee5b.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+2$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+2$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/14/144142b823e7db82eb9b65e6ddc835d75a7fd8d218eab17f9db9019c4b8ab109.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/a2/a2c948117d63fdd60d16ac29b3900238f7c246201bd45df3e3ee940aac0d4ee6.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(0,0)=2 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(1,0)=3 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(0,1)=3 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(1,1)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =2+3+3+4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566135 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566275 1069 "q-93419" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566275 1071 "Which of the following is **not** the net of a prism?" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566275 1072 #uuid "ff018c25-43d3-5a1d-9dbc-90c62eb5eb4d" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566275 1074 "![](images/d9/d9010650127d8dcfcd387df09b9dce689ece90e3912ede882241c8b714f91d45.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nIf we were to fold the net along its edges to form a solid shape, we'd get a triangular pyramid.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/6b/6bf9ac2bd130fdad3a056d0e5680e010003366f115e3655de59bbd795d1513f5.png)\n\nSo, the net that does not correspond to a prism is:\n\n![](images/f5/f5be9bb97784c25d555d4581bc67916ab0b8e57ade33619b3df27d2eb0f59db2.png)\n\nThe rest of the given nets will give us a (triangular or rectangular) prism." 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566276 1062 #uuid "092d0805-5447-576b-a17e-54b3ad43c01d" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566276 1063 #edb/ref 17592186566222 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566277 1058 #edb/ref 17592186566282 13194139536104 17592186045